# BUG-310 — File.append silently TRUNCATED the file when the read failed for any reason
# other than the file being absent.
#
# Append is read-existing + concat + rewrite (Zig 0.16 removed File.seekFromEnd), and the
# rewrite uses createFile, which TRUNCATES. The read was `catch ""`, catching everything --
# so an AccessDenied, a SharingViolation or a transient I/O error produced an empty
# `existing`, and the file was left holding only the appended fragment. Now only
# FileNotFound yields "", and anything else refuses rather than truncating.
#
# WHAT THIS FIXTURE CANNOT DO: it does not reproduce the data loss. Provoking a read
# failure that is not FileNotFound needs a lock or a permission denial, neither of which is
# reachable from Zebra source here. It is a CONTROL on the fix.
#
# THE CONTROL IS NOT DECORATION. The fix narrows a catch-all to one specific error, and the
# obvious way to get that wrong is to narrow it too far -- to stop tolerating the absent
# file, which is the case the `catch ""` existed for in the first place. Leg 1 is exactly
# that case, and it would fail loudly against an over-narrowed fix.
def main()
    var p = "bug310_scratch.txt"

    # Start clean; delete tolerates absence (BUG-307).
    File.delete(p)

    # leg 1: append to a file that does NOT exist must create it, not refuse.
    File.append(p, "one")
    if File.read(p) <> "one"
        print("FAIL: append to a missing file must create it")
        File.delete(p)
        return

    # leg 2: append to an EXISTING file must PRESERVE what was there. This is the
    # property the truncation bug destroyed.
    File.append(p, "-two")
    if File.read(p) <> "one-two"
        print("FAIL: append truncated the existing contents")
        File.delete(p)
        return

    File.delete(p)
    print("bug310: OK")
